          "One of the reasons the question “Can Breast Pump Cause Cancer?” creates so much anxiety is that several common breastfeeding-related breast conditions can closely resemble the symptoms of breast cancer. As a Medical Oncologist, I frequently meet women who are alarmed after discovering a lump or experiencing breast pain while breastfeeding. The good news is that most breast lumps in breastfeeding women are benign (non-cancerous) and are often related to milk production rather than cancer. However, it is equally important not to ignore persistent symptoms, which is why understanding these conditions is essential.",
          "A blocked milk duct is one of the most common reasons mothers feel a breast lump. This occurs when milk fails to drain properly from one of the milk ducts, resulting in a small, tender lump. The affected area may feel firm and painful but usually improves with frequent breastfeeding or pumping, gentle massage, warm compresses, and proper breast emptying. Although a blocked duct can be uncomfortable, it does not increase the risk of breast cancer or support the myth that Can Breast Pump Cause Cancer .",
          "Another common condition is mastitis , an inflammation of the breast tissue that may or may not involve a bacterial infection. Studies suggest that 10–20% of breastfeeding mothers experience mastitis, particularly during the first three months after childbirth. Symptoms include breast pain, redness, warmth, swelling, fever, and flu-like illness. Because the breast may become swollen and tender, many women mistakenly believe they have cancer. Fortunately, mastitis usually responds well to antibiotics, continued breastfeeding or pumping, adequate hydration, and rest.",
